lisa-design:
- CountStatusesForFilterSets keeps its (streams, sets) signature and builds the label index itself, lazily on the first scoped filter: one index per stream set, no index when no set is scoped, and no churn for its callers and tests.
- The JSON scope memo in matchingStreams is kept: at 1,000 filters x 10k streams with ~50% shared filters, dropping it was ~8% faster (16.2 vs 17.6 ms) but doubled memory (19.2 vs 9.3 MB/op), and duplicate scopes are common in real profiles.
- GetEvidenceStatusCountsByFilters (no callers today) now delegates to CountStatusesForFilterSets with one single-filter set per id instead of its own loop. A single filter has identical semantics there (nil scope = every stream); rows come back sorted by status, which was map order before.
- relational.NewLatestStreamIndex is the one place that turns latest streams into a labelfilter.Index; lineage loadCompliance uses it too.
- Accepted difference from MatchLabels: an unknown query operator always errors, even behind a short-circuit (documented on labelfilter.Index).
